     <article class="story single" data-beat="world">
       <div class="meta"><span class="beat-tag">WORLD</span> · The Hindu World · 2026-10-15 · editor 9/10 · 2 min read <span class="badge ok">fact-checked</span><span class="badge conf" title="Reported by The Hindu World, Al Jazeera, News18 World">confirmed by 3 sources</span></div>
       <h1>34 Children, Teacher Killed in Peshawar School Roof Collapse</h1>
       <div class="tags"><span>#Pakistan</span> <span>#School Collapse</span> <span>#Peshawar</span></div>
       <p>At least 34 children and a teacher were killed when the roof of a private school collapsed on Thursday, October 15, 2026, in Peshawar, the capital of Pakistan’s Khyber Pakhtunkhwa province. The incident occurred in Dir Colony, a congested inner city area, according to Peshawar Capital City Police chief Mian Saeed. Hospital authorities and police confirmed the fatalities.</p><img class="hero-art" src="/img/2026-10-15/34-children-teacher-killed-in-peshawar-school-roof-collapse.webp" width="1200" height="675" alt="" loading="eager" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async"><p>According to The Hindu World, 30 students were present in classrooms on the upper floor of the Al-Khair School when the three-storey building's poorly constructed roof gave way. The owner, who operated the institution as an education trust, has been arrested. Lady Reading Hospital (LRH) in Peshawar received 32 children and one teacher dead, as stated by hospital spokesperson Muhammad Asim. Two additional bodies were transferred to Naseerullah Khan Babar Memorial Hospital. Al Jazeera reported at least 29 people, mostly children, were killed, while News18 World cited 21 fatalities, including 20 students aged seven to 12 and a female teacher, in Peshawar's Afridi Abad locality.</p><p>Twenty injured children were also brought to LRH, with most discharged after first aid; however, three critically injured children with head injuries were admitted. An emergency was declared at the hospital for the injured, all of whom were age 13 or younger. Peshawar Deputy Commissioner Afaq Wazir confirmed all trapped individuals were rescued and ordered a probe. Provincial Education Secretary Inayatullah Waseem directed the Private Schools Regulatory Authority to submit a report by Friday, October 16, 2026. The city police chief noted narrow streets hindered rescue operations, adding that authorities would examine building regulations and investigate negligence.</p><p>Pakistan Army personnel, alongside other rescue and relief agencies, participated in the operation. Khyber Pakhtunkhwa Inspector General of Police (IGP) Zulfiqar Hameed directed a green corridor for ambulances. President Asif Ali Zardari, Prime Minister Shehbaz Sharif, National Assembly Speaker Sardar Ayaz Sadiq, KPK Governor Faisal Karim Kundi, and Pakistan Peoples Party (PPP) Chairman Bilawal Bhutto Zardari expressed deep grief and condolences. Building and roof collapses are common in Pakistan, often linked to poor safety standards and substandard construction materials.</p><p>What to watch: The outcome of the official probes into building regulations and negligence.</p>
       <p class="critique">Editor's note: The draft effectively synthesizes the source material, incorporating specific figures, official responses, and contextual details while acknowledging other reporting.</p>
       <div class="provenance">
      <b>How this was reported · 3 sources</b>
      <ul><li><a href="https://www.thehindu.com/news/international/students-teacher-killed-in-school-roof-collapse-in-pakistan-peshwar/article71586942.ece" rel="noopener">The Hindu World</a></li><li><a href="https://www.aljazeera.com/video/newsfeed/2026/10/15/10-15-pak-school-collapse-sv?traffic_source=rss" rel="noopener">Al Jazeera</a></li><li><a href="https://www.news18.com/world/pakistan-peshawar-school-roof-collapse-18-killed-17-students-10382915.html" rel="noopener">News18 World</a></li></ul>
      <p>Synthesised from 3 independent reports on the same event. Details are attributed to the outlet that carried them; nothing outside these sources is published.</p>
    </div>
       
