     <article class="story single" data-beat="health">
       <div class="meta"><span class="beat-tag">HEALTH</span> · ET Healthworld · 2026-10-15 · editor 9/10 · 1 min read <span class="badge ok">fact-checked</span><span class="badge quick">quick bite</span></div>
       <h1>Health Ministry Asked to Review Stent Safety After Sri Lanka Withdrawal</h1>
       <div class="tags"><span>#Health Ministry</span> <span>#Coronary Stents</span> <span>#Medical Devices</span> <span>#Sri Lanka</span></div>
       <p>The Health Ministry has been urged to investigate safety concerns regarding certain India-made coronary stents. This request was made in a representation submitted to the cabinet secretariat.</p><img class="hero-art" src="/img/2026-10-15/health-ministry-asked-to-review-stent-safety-after-sri-lanka.webp" width="1200" height="675" alt="" loading="eager" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async"><p>Rajya Sabha member Ajeet Madhavrao Gopchade, a medical doctor, flagged these concerns. His representation specifically highlighted three models of India-made, drug-eluting coronary stents.</p><p>These particular stent models were reportedly withdrawn from use in Sri Lanka. The withdrawal followed documented quality issues, prompting the call for examination by the Indian Health Ministry.</p><p>What to watch: The Health Ministry's response to the representation.</p>
       <p class="critique">Editor's note: The draft concisely and accurately captures all key elements of the provided source summary without unnecessary filler.</p>
       <div class="provenance">
      <b>Source</b>
      <ul><li><a href="https://health.economictimes.indiatimes.com/news/policy/health-ministry-asked-to-examine-coronary-stent-safety-concerns/134955255" rel="noopener">ET Healthworld</a></li></ul>
      <p>AI-generated and fact-checked against the original report; claims the gate cannot verify are held back.</p>
    </div>
       
