     <article class="story single" data-beat="positive">
       <div class="meta"><span class="beat-tag">POSITIVE</span> · The Better India · 2026-10-15 · editor 10/10 · 3 min read <span class="badge ok">fact-checked</span></div>
       <h1>Kinnauri Midwife's Life Inspires India's First Narrative Film in Local Language</h1>
       <div class="tags"><span>#Culture</span> <span>#Film</span> <span>#Kinnauri</span> <span>#Community</span></div>
       <p>The life of Bua Dolma, a midwife who tirelessly walked a punishing, hour-long path daily to Kandhar village in Kinnaur, has inspired &quot;Tenfa,&quot; India's first narrative film in the Kinnauri language. Kandhar, now abandoned due to landslides and dam construction, represented a community where Bua Dolma's services were essential due to the absence of hospitals or roads. Her niece, filmmaker Nihaarika Negi, transformed these family memories and Kinnaur's cultural heritage into the film, emphasizing that it truly &quot;takes a village to make a film.&quot;</p><img class="hero-art" src="/img/2026-10-15/kinnauri-midwifes-life-inspires-indias-first-narrative-film-.webp" width="1200" height="675" alt="" loading="eager" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async"><p>Nihaarika, who returns to her family's home in Sungra, Nitschar Valley, almost every year since 2022, developed the film's concept during research for a documentary theatre project on climate change and indigenous identity. Collaborating with the theatre collective Asagri Jagash and trans-led studio Starhopper, she found Bua Dolma's revered life to be the emotional core of the story. Her other aunt, Chachi, portrays Bua Dolma on screen, with old footage of Chachi dancing even making its way into the final cut. The script was shared with local cultural organizations like Z-Tales and composer Satish Uncle for community feedback, ensuring its authenticity.</p><p>Every actor in &quot;Tenfa&quot; is a non-professional from the local community, a deliberate choice by Nihaarika to ensure the film resonated with their lived experiences. The most challenging role to cast was the grandmother, ultimately played by village elder Appi. As Appi had never learned to read and only knew spoken Kinnauri, a system was devised: Nihaarika explained scenes to Chachi, who translated for Appi, and lines were taught through call-and-response. An off-camera prompt ensured smooth delivery during the shoot, with the cast quickly adapting to filmmaking's unique grammar.</p><p>Chachi played a pivotal role behind the scenes, managing resources and rallying community support in Sungra. The six-day shoot across sixteen locations in Kinnaur's challenging, &quot;up-and-down&quot; terrain involved carrying equipment by hand. A memorable scene on the Rarang slope saw the crew sliding and gripping trees to maintain position. Line producer Mahesh Negi ensured a &quot;green shoot,&quot; prioritizing natural light, minimal equipment, and local resources, with the palki built by local craftsmen, costumes by a village tailor, and food prepared by local women.</p><p>Initially a straightforward narrative, &quot;Tenfa&quot; evolved in the edit to become a hybrid of narrative and archival footage, incorporating Nihaarika's personal recordings since 2022 and historical material from archive.org. This transformation aimed to capture the specific weight of Kinnaur's changing landscape—a region facing environmental shifts and dam construction along the Sutlej River, with an estimated 140 dams built or planned. Nihaarika describes the film as a &quot;time capsule&quot; of her family, village, and Kinnaur at a critical juncture, questioning unsustainable development while celebrating the region's rich cultural heritage.</p><p>The film's collective spirit extended to its screenings, with nearly a thousand people, including many schoolchildren, attending thirteen viewings across Delhi, Chandigarh, Shimla, and Kinnaur. In Kinnaur, lacking a cinema hall, the team used a portable projector in schools and gram panchayats. Screenings often became impromptu community events, like a two-hour concert in Sangla. Nihaarika observed children from archival footage watching themselves as toddlers and young students showing keen interest in filmmaking, highlighting the film's impact and the evolving media literacy of &quot;Gen Alpha.&quot;</p><p>Nihaarika reflects that &quot;Tenfa&quot; challenged her usual &quot;edgy&quot; artistic instincts, demanding simplicity and responsiveness to the material rather than a rigid intellectual vision. She likens the entire experience to a transformative trek up a mountain. For aspiring filmmakers, her advice is to start with available resources, clarify their message, create and share work to build confidence, and actively seek connections and opportunities in an industry she describes as having &quot;collapsed&quot; in traditional business models. Her next project, &quot;Feral,&quot; is a psychological horror film and an Indo-German co-production.</p><p>Why it matters: Preserving indigenous culture and language through community-driven cinema.</p>
       <p class="critique">Editor's note: The article thoroughly captures the production details, community involvement, and thematic depth of the film project as presented in the source.</p>
       <div class="provenance">
