     <article class="story single" data-beat="business">
       <div class="meta"><span class="beat-tag">BUSINESS</span> · Economic Times · 2026-10-15 · editor 10/10 · 2 min read <span class="badge ok">fact-checked</span></div>
       <h1>Michelle Obama's Girls Opportunity Alliance Pledges $1 Million, Expands Global Education Efforts</h1>
       <div class="tags"><span>#Philanthropy</span> <span>#Education</span> <span>#Girls Empowerment</span> <span>#Michelle Obama</span></div>
       <p>Michelle Obama's Girls Opportunity Alliance has pledged over $1 million to support 25 organizations across 15 countries, intensifying efforts to empower girls globally through education. This initiative focuses on helping adolescent girls access education, overcome challenges, and build better futures. In addition to this pledge, the alliance announced a $2.5 million fundraising plan in October 2025, aiming to support 54 grassroots organizations across 28 countries working to enhance educational opportunities for girls.</p><img class="hero-art" src="/img/2026-10-15/michelle-obamas-girls-opportunity-alliance-pledges-1-million.webp" width="1200" height="675" alt="" loading="eager" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async"><p>In a new video, Michelle Obama emphasized the importance of these investments, stating, “I’ve always believed that every girl holds a unique promise — a special way that she can change the lives of those around her, maybe even the world. And that’s why investing in girls isn’t just the right thing to do. It’s an investment in our shared future.” Organizations receiving support from the $1 million funding include Associação Mempodera in Brazil, which promotes gender equality through sports and education; Girl Power Foundation in Uganda, aiding teenage girls with mentoring; and Milaan Foundation in India, boosting confidence and opportunities for teenage girls. Specific programs include Girls First Initiative in Tanzania setting up leadership clubs in four schools, and Haiti Adolescent Girls Network training community mentors and establishing 10 spaces for weekly educational sessions.</p><p>The $2.5 million initiative, part of the Obama Foundation, aims to help girls aged 10 to 19 overcome educational barriers and complete their schooling. This funding will support scholarships, educational materials, mentorship, and after-school activities, while also addressing critical challenges such as child marriage, sexual abuse, and financial difficulties. Eligible organizations can apply for grants up to $50,000 for specific projects, and community leaders receive training, networking opportunities, and assistance in raising additional funds. These efforts build on previous commitments, including over $500,000 pledged in 2019 from sales of Obama’s memoir, *Becoming*, for girls’ education, and an earlier $500,000 provided this year to 10 organizations in Chicago to expand their programs.</p><p>Beyond girls' education, Michelle Obama has a history of significant philanthropic endeavors. In 2014, she announced a philanthropic initiative, the Veterans Philanthropy Exchange, pledging over $100 million to assist military families over five years. Unveiled with Dr. Jill Biden, this program marked the third anniversary of Joining Forces, an initiative they co-founded to mobilize support for service members and their families. At the time, participating groups had already committed $62 million to military families by 2019, and the new pledge amounted to $102 million in additional funding over the subsequent five years.</p>
       <p class="critique">Editor's note: The draft effectively synthesizes the full text provided, maintaining accuracy and covering all key philanthropic details.</p>
       <div class="provenance">
      <b>Source</b>
      <ul><li><a href="https://economictimes.indiatimes.com/news/international/world-news/michelle-obamas-1-million-pledge-to-support-25-organizations-across-15-countries-plus-her-2-5-million-plan-to-help-girls-worldwide/articleshow/134970087.cms" rel="noopener">Economic Times</a></li></ul>
      <p>AI-generated and fact-checked against the original report; claims the gate cannot verify are held back.</p>
