     <article class="story single" data-beat="cricket">
       <div class="meta"><span class="beat-tag">CRICKET</span> · Crictracker · 2026-10-15 · editor 10/10 · 3 min read <span class="badge ok">fact-checked</span><span class="badge conf" title="Reported by Crictracker, ESPN Cricinfo">confirmed by 2 sources</span></div>
       <h1>Punam Raut Announces Retirement from International Cricket After 17-Year Career</h1>
       <div class="tags"><span>#Punam Raut</span> <span>#Cricket</span> <span>#International Retirement</span> <span>#India Women's Cricket</span></div>
       <p>Punam Raut, the former India opener, officially announced her retirement from all forms of international cricket on Thursday, October 15, 2026, sharing the news through an Instagram statement. This decision brings a close to an extensive career that spanned more than 17 years at the highest level. Her international journey commenced with an ODI debut against West Indies in March 2009, and she played her final international match, an ODI against England, in June 2021. Throughout this period, Raut solidified her reputation as a dependable top-order batter, representing India in three ODI World Cups and four T20 World Cups, a fact also highlighted by ESPN Cricinfo.</p><img class="hero-art" src="/img/2026-10-15/punam-raut-announces-retirement-from-international-cricket-a.webp" width="1200" height="675" alt="" loading="eager" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async"><p>Across formats, Raut amassed significant career statistics. She accumulated 2,299 runs in 73 One Day Internationals at an average of 34.83, including three centuries and 15 fifties. In 35 T20 International appearances, she scored 719 runs. Her Test career, comprising four matches, yielded 264 runs, with a career-best score of 130 achieved against South Africa in 2014. A definitive highlight of her career was the 2017 Women's World Cup, where Raut scored 381 runs. This included a century of 109 against Ireland, during which she and Deepti Sharma established a record 320-run opening partnership, a detail confirmed by both Crictracker and ESPN Cricinfo. She also made a crucial contribution of 86 runs in the final against England at Lord's, where India narrowly missed the title.</p><p>Beyond the statistics, Raut's career also featured a notable act of sportsmanship during the day-night Test against Australia in 2021. In that match, where she scored 36 in the first innings, she chose to walk off the field after edging a delivery to the keeper, despite the on-field umpire having ruled in her favour. This moment was widely praised as a display of integrity. Raut, who turned 37 on October 14, 2026, expressed profound gratitude in her retirement statement, reflecting on her journey: &quot;From the streets of Mumbai to wearing the Indian jersey, this journey has been nothing short of a blessing. A journey filled with sacrifices, struggles, unforgettable victories, tough lessons, and moments I will cherish for a lifetime. Representing India was never just a dream fulfilled; it was a privilege I carried with immense pride.&quot;</p><p>Looking to the future, Raut has made it clear that her association with cricket will continue beyond her playing days. In her Instagram statement, she articulated her commitment to giving back to the sport: &quot;As I close this chapter of my international cricket career, my heart is filled with gratitude for everyone who believed in me, stood by me, and became a part of my journey. Cricket gave me more than a career. It gave me a purpose, an identity, and a family.&quot; She concluded with a clear vision: &quot;The player in me may step aside, but the love for this game will never retire. My next innings is about giving back to the game that gave me everything.&quot; While her specific plans remain unconfirmed, her statement signals a continued dedication to the game.</p>
       <p class="critique">Editor's note: Comprehensive, well-structured, and includes all relevant biographical and career statistics provided in the source material.</p>
       <div class="provenance">
      <b>How this was reported · 2 sources</b>
      <ul><li><a href="https://www.crictracker.com/cricket-news/punam-raut-announces-retirement-from-all-forms-of-international-cricket/" rel="noopener">Crictracker</a></li><li><a href="https://www.cricinfo.com/ci/content/story/1558630.html?ex_cid=OTC-RSS" rel="noopener">ESPN Cricinfo</a></li></ul>
      <p>Synthesised from 2 independent reports on the same event. Details are attributed to the outlet that carried them; nothing outside these sources is published.</p>
