     <article class="story single" data-beat="tech">
       <div class="meta"><span class="beat-tag">TECH</span> · TechCrunch · 2026-10-15 · editor 9/10 · 2 min read <span class="badge ok">fact-checked</span></div>
       <h1>Ricursive Intelligence Founders Detail AI-Designed Chips to Tackle Compute Crisis</h1>
       <div class="tags"><span>#AI</span> <span>#Chip Design</span> <span>#Ricursive Intelligence</span> <span>#Compute Crisis</span></div>
       <p>Ricursive Intelligence founders Anna Goldie and Azalia Mirhoseini presented their vision at TechCrunch Disrupt on Wednesday, asserting that more efficient AI chip design is key to resolving the burgeoning compute crisis. Their startup is advancing artificial intelligence capable of designing chips, learning from each iteration, and refining subsequent designs, with the overarching aim of automating and accelerating the entire chip design process. Goldie highlighted the urgency, stating that demand for compute is projected to increase at least 10,000-fold in the next five years, a figure she suggested might even be conservative.</p><img class="hero-art" src="/img/2026-10-15/ricursive-intelligence-founders-detail-ai-designed-chips-to-.webp" width="1200" height="675" alt="" loading="eager" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async"><p>The company's strategy focuses on creating chips that are more efficient in utilizing data center bandwidth, wafers, and fabs, thereby mitigating the need for a proportional expansion of physical infrastructure. Goldie posits that by entrusting AI with chip design, human engineers can redirect their efforts towards developing innovative applications and exploring new computational possibilities. She articulated this shift by noting that &quot;humans come bring their great ideas and creative thoughts, and execution is handled by AI,&quot; a trend she observes across chip design, software development, and other sectors.</p><p>Ricursive Intelligence's approach challenges conventional chip design methodologies by enabling AI to discover unconventional layouts that can surpass traditional designs. As an illustration, AlphaChip, an AI system previously developed by the founders, generated a circular, &quot;donut-shaped&quot; arrangement of chip components. This design, initially surprising to engineers, ultimately proved successful. The founders explained that their AI optimizes entire chips, moving away from the traditional practice of segmenting them into numerous smaller blocks, which facilitates the creation of intricate patterns, including wave-like designs, about which they plan to disclose further details.</p><p>Goldie and Mirhoseini also contend that AI-designed chips will unlock the creation of novel chip types previously unfeasible. Goldie pointed out that traditional chip design cycles, which can span from six months to two years, necessitate extreme selectivity. However, if AI can design a chip in &quot;like a week,&quot; it opens the door to custom chips for a diverse array of applications in fields such as space, augmented and virtual reality (AR/VR), and biological computing, making such endeavors financially viable and more energy efficient.</p><p>What to watch: Further developments in AI-driven chip design and its impact on computational efficiency.</p>
       <p class="critique">Editor's note: The draft effectively covers the founders' presentation, the technical goals of their AI, and the business/engineering context provided in the source.</p>
       <div class="provenance">
      <b>Source</b>
      <ul><li><a href="https://techcrunch.com/2026/10/14/ricursive-intelligence-is-betting-on-ai-designed-chips-to-help-solve-the-compute-crisis/" rel="noopener">TechCrunch</a></li></ul>
      <p>AI-generated and fact-checked against the original report; claims the gate cannot verify are held back.</p>
    </div>
       
