       <div class="meta"><span class="beat-tag">NATIONAL</span> · Times of India · 2026-10-15 · editor 9/10 · 1 min read <span class="badge ok">fact-checked</span></div>
       <h1>US Data Centre Expansion Sparks Bipartisan Community Opposition</h1>
       <div class="tags"><span>#national</span> <span>#brief</span></div>
       <p>Across the United States, rapid data centre expansion is triggering a wave of local opposition, uniting Democrats and Republicans. Residents are protesting against significant noise, high electricity demand, and intensive water usage, with approximately 640,000 people now involved in the movement. Over 200 new opposition groups formed between June and August, reflecting a broader concern regarding corporate influence and limited public input on development decisions.</p><img class="hero-art" src="/img/2026-10-15/us-data-centre-expansion-sparks-bipartisan-community-opposit.webp" width="1200" height="675" alt="" loading="eager" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async"><p>While developers promise tax revenue and construction jobs, critics argue that permanent employment figures are low, often ranging between 100 and 200 jobs per facility. The backlash is now influencing political agendas, as officials face pressure to slow expansion and increase transparency regarding project agreements.</p>
       <p class="critique">Editor's note: The brief effectively captures the core conflict and provides relevant statistics despite the source being a longer, more detailed report.</p>
       <div class="provenance">
      <b>Source</b>
      <ul><li><a href="https://timesofindia.indiatimes.com/world/us/us-mid-term-elections/news/how-opposition-to-data-centres-is-bringing-democrats-and-republicans-together-during-midterms/articleshow/134957287.cms" rel="noopener">Times of India</a></li></ul>
      <p>AI-generated and fact-checked against the original report; claims the gate cannot verify are held back.</p>
    </div>
       
