     <article class="story single" data-beat="positive">
       <div class="meta"><span class="beat-tag">POSITIVE</span> · The Better India · 2026-10-15 · editor 9/10 · 2 min read <span class="badge ok">fact-checked</span></div>
       <h1>Zawar Transforms Mining Town into Rajasthan's Football Hub</h1>
       <div class="tags"><span>#Football</span> <span>#Youth Development</span> <span>#Rajasthan</span> <span>#Community Impact</span></div>
       <p>Zawar, a remote mining township in southern Rajasthan, is steadily transforming into a vibrant football hub thanks to the Zinc Football programme, which commenced in 2017. This initiative has already seen over 5,000 children pass through its ranks, with many achieving significant milestones, including representing India and signing with professional clubs such as Ashish Chetry (South United FC), Rajrup Sarkar (NorthEast United FC), Prem Hansdak (Mohun Bagan), Mohammed Kaif (Hyderabad FC), Sahil Poonia (Bengaluru FC), and Akshat (NorthEast United FC).</p><img class="hero-art" src="/img/2026-10-15/zawar-transforms-mining-town-into-rajasthans-football-hub.webp" width="1200" height="675" alt="" loading="eager" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async"><p>The academy operates on a unique model designed to eliminate barriers for rural children, covering all expenses including fees, equipment, hostel accommodation, and food, ensuring parents spend &quot;not even one rupee.&quot; Children from farming households, tribal communities, and low-income families, who previously found organized sport out of reach, now benefit from artificial turf, recovery rooms, performance analysis technology, licensed coaches, and residential facilities. The program is also integrated with education, with the campus built adjacent to a local school, fostering a holistic development environment.</p><p>The Zinc Football programme currently houses 70 residential players across Under-15 girls, Under-18 boys, and Under-23 boys groups. Complementing this, Zinc Football Community Schools across Rajasthan train over 300 children weekly. Notable successes include Mohammed Kaif, who earned four international caps for India at the Under-16 level, and Sahil Poonia, the first academy player to wear the India jersey, later winning Best Goalkeeper at the SAFF U-17 Championship. The girls' programme is also thriving, with scholarship holders like Mina Kumari Meena, Sonam Ghosh, and Bindiya Kumari Meena demonstrating strong performances and aspiring to play for India.</p><p>Ayonava Bagchi, a senior member of the club, highlights the profound impact beyond trophies, noting that &quot;When one child reaches that level, the whole village starts believing.&quot; Priya Agarwal Hebbar, Chairperson of Hindustan Zinc, also expressed that &quot;The most rewarding part of this journey has been watching our champions, especially our girls, grow into confident role models who inspire the next generation to dream bigger.&quot; The recent launch of Rajasthan's first fully residential girls' football academy further underscores the program's commitment to addressing critical gaps in grassroots sports, offering a pathway to a future shaped by possibility for many children.</p><p>Why it matters: The Zinc Football programme offers a replicable model for grassroots sports development and empowerment in underserved communities.</p>
       <p class="critique">Editor's note: The draft effectively summarizes the Zinc Football initiative and its impact on the Zawar community.</p>
       <div class="provenance">
      <b>Source</b>
      <ul><li><a href="https://thebetterindia.com/sports/how-zawar-rajasthan-zinc-football-academy-produced-5000-footballers-12678737" rel="noopener">The Better India</a></li></ul>
      <p>AI-generated and fact-checked against the original report; claims the gate cannot verify are held back.</p>
    </div>
       
